  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Fairly seasonable late November temperatures will \ncontinue through the end of the week. A widespread, soaking rainfall \nwill arrive later Thanksgiving Day, with unsettled weather then \nlasting into the weekend.\n\nStrong high pressure over the western Great Lakes this afternoon, \nwill slowly drift east across the eastern Great Lakes into New \nEngland by late Wednesday providing dry weather through midweek.\nHigh pressure will then anchor itself across New England Wednesday \nnight, while burgeoning low pressure starts to form just off the \nSoutheast coast.\n\nNorthern and southern stream energy will phase with a large upper \nlevel low taking shape over the Tennessee Valley, as broad surface \nlow pressure tracks north into the Carolinas Wednesday night with a \ntrough extending westward into the lower Ohio Valley. The system is \nthen forecast to continue northward into the eastern Great Lakes and \nNortheast later Thursday into Friday. There is high confidence that \nwidespread precipitation will spread northward into the region on \nThursday as the deformation region works through with an easterly \nfeed of moisture via the system's warm conveyor. Some uncertainty \nstill exists with the exact onset time, although with additional \nguidance suites (including some hi-res) now within this forecast \nperiod and with the presence of the strong high anchored over New \nEngland, consensus is leaning toward a later start to the steady \nrain. At this point, after a dry Thanksgiving morning, rain looks to \nget into the southern Tier sometime after midday/early afternoon, \nthen slowly spread northward through the remainder of the day into \nearly evening. Areas east of Lake America even stand the chance to \nremain rain free through at least the daylight hours. Strong \neasterly jet aloft and tight surface pressure gradient will also \nyield breezy to windy conditions Thursday afternoon through Thursday \nnight, though winds are expected to remain below advisory criteria \nat this time.\n\nTemperatures aloft remain quite mild with profiles suggesting almost \nall rain as the primary precipitation type through Friday morning. \nThough confidence remains very low for any wintry precipitation with \ntemps aloft well above freezing during this time, very low dew \npoints at the onset of precipitation may allow wet-bulbing effect to \nvery briefly bring temperatures down near the freezing mark, \nbringing the potential for a small window of sleet or freezing rain \nin pockets across the higher terrain areas before quickly switching \nover to plain rain. Will continue to monitor for any changes.\n\nFriday through Saturday, occasional showers will continue as the \ndeep mid level closed low crosses the region. The airmass will grow \ncold enough for some lake enhancement by Friday and Saturday, with\nearly indications pointing towards westerly flow during this time \nframe, gradually becoming southwesterly late Saturday into Saturday \nnight. Late Friday through Saturday, modest cold advection will drop \n850 MB temperatures down to -2C to -3C. Profiles suggest rain for \nlower elevations, but some wet snow may start to mix in across \nhigher terrain. Marginal temperatures at the surface and aloft \nsuggest there will be little, if any snow accumulation during this \nperiod, even across higher terrain. Best chance for a light \naccumulation would be across the higher terrain of the western \nSouthern Tier.\n\nThis system will bring beneficial rain to the area, with rainfall \namounts likely reaching 1.0\" to 1.5\" across much of the region late \nThursday through Saturday with the higher-end amounts expected \nacross far western NY. With much of the area in D1 moderate drought \nafter the exceptionally dry conditions since September, this is \nexpected to be a beneficial rainfall with minimal hydro concerns at \nthis time.",
